WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.340
Hey there, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 53 for May 28th, 2026.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:11.920
Today's AI news is pretty exciting, and I promise no jargon, let's go.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:18.060
So imagine you could start a song as a gentle opera and have a turn into heavy metal halfway

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.940
through without any awkward jump.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:26.480
That is exactly what 11 labs just made possible with their new tool called Music V2.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.820
It is an AI model that builds full songs and lets you change entire music styles right in the

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:38.240
middle of one track. Think of it like a talented friend who can rewrite a story on the fly,

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:44.100
keeping the main characters and plot the same while swapping the setting from a quiet library to a

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:50.360
wild concert. The model holds onto the melody, the beat, and the overall feeling even as the

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:56.180
instruments and mood shift completely. It also includes something called in-painting which works

00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:01.540
like editing just one paragraph in an essay while the rest of the paper stays untouched.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:08.060
You pick one section of the song, and the AI rewrites only that part so it still fits with everything

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:14.280
around it. This matters because music creation used to need years of practice or expensive gear,

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:20.980
but now anyone with a phone can experiment. A student making a school video could start with a simple

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:27.700
tune and turn it into a full band sound without hiring musicians. Or a creator on TikTok could

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:33.180
build background tracks that change mood to match different parts of their clip. The result feels

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:38.080
polished because the AI learned from millions of real song show notes and rhythms connect.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:44.200
You can try it yourself right now by heading to the 11 labs website and using their music demo.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:49.860
Type a short prompt like upbeat guitar riff that turns into orchestral strings and hearth

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:55.740
the style switch while the main tune stays recognizable. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:01.660
where we slow down and see exactly how this kind of AI actually works. Think of it like the auto

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:06.920
complete on your phone when you text a friend. Your phone guesses the next word because it has seen

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:13.740
billions of messages and learned common patterns. Music V2 do the same thing, except it guesses the

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:19.560
next sound instead of the next word. The AI has listened to millions of songs so it knows which

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:25.300
notes beats and instruments usually follow each other. When you ask it to switch from operate to

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:31.500
metal, it does not pick one note at a time and hope for the best. It plans several seconds ahead,

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:36.620
almost like a storyteller who already knows the ending of the chapter before writing the middle.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:41.840
That forward planning keeps the melody from falling apart when the style changes.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.960
In painting works the same way but on a smaller scale, like fixing one scene in a movie while

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:53.980
making sure it still matches the scenes before and after. The model treats the whole song like one

00:02:53.980 --> 00:03:00.200
connected story where every part has to fit with what comes next. And that is basically how this AI

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:05.900
music generation works. Not so mysterious once you see the auto complete idea underneath it all, right?

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:12.560
Let us move to some cool tools you can actually touch today. YouTube is rolling out an automatic system

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:16.780
that spots when videos use AI to create or heavily change the footage.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:22.820
The labels will show up right below longer videos and as an overlay on short clips so viewers know

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:28.440
what they are watching. Think of it like those nutrition labels on food packages that tell you what

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:33.960
is really inside. This helps when you are learning from videos or sharing clips with friends

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:40.120
because you can tell real recordings from made up scenes. Go to YouTube on your phone or laptop.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:46.420
Search for a recent short video and watch for any new label that says altered or synthetic content.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:53.200
It is already appearing on some clips so you can see the difference right away. Next up, Taylor Swift is

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:59.560
taking a legal step to protect her voice and face from A-fakes. She is registering trademarks on her

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:05.100
likeness specifically to stop deep fakes that could make it look like she is endorsing products she

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:11.500
never touched. Copyright law alone was not enough so this gives her stronger tools to block fake

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:18.320
endorsements. Imagine someone using your school photo in an ad without asking. The same technology

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:24.100
that creates celebrity fakes could eventually reach regular people too. Reading about how public

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:29.860
figures are pushing for better protections is a good way to understand why rules around AI content

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:36.980
keep changing. Time for a couple of quick bits that caught my eye. Lawmakers in Illinois just passed

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:42.560
a bill that would require big AI companies to let outside experts check whether they are following

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:47.980
safety standards. The governor plans to sign it, making it one of the strongest state level AI

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:54.440
laws so far. It shows governments starting to treat AI safety like they treat food or car safety.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:05:00.140
Someone recently put the newest version of chat gpt into a conversation with clever bot,

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:06.540
an old internet chat bot from almost 20 years ago. The transcript is funny because the newer model

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:11.120
responds with personality and context while the older one jumps around randomly.

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:17.940
It is a quick way to see how far conversational AI has come in two decades and that is a wrap.

